    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Supported-metal emissions control catalysts must maintain steady activity at high temperatures over their entire lifetimes. However, preventing loss of reactive surface area due to particle sintering is challenging. It is thought that spatially isolating metal nanoparticles on the catalyst support slows high-temperature degradation by minimizing the probability of interparticle interaction. Yet, studying this effect is complicated by an inability to synthesize catalysts with well-defined nanoparticle spatial distributions using traditional synthesis methods. Herein, we prepare Pd/SiO​2 catalysts with quantifiable nanoparticle spatial distributions using colloidal Pd nanoparticles and amorphous spherical silica supports. With this model system, we demonstrate that Pd/SiO​2 sintering is thermally activated at 888°C and is independent of nanoparticle density on the support surface over two orders of magnitude. The vapor pressure of Pd and corresponding NP atomic solid-vapor exchange rate suggest a gas-phase sintering mechanism activated at the reduction temperature of PdO to metallic Pd. While minimal fusion of adjacent nanoparticles occurs at temperatures below 888 °C, the loss of reactive surface area is negligible. These results call into question the traditional approach of achieving maximum stability via spatial isolation of nanoparticles. Moreover, the remarkable stability of our system up to 888°C unlocks new strategies for the realization of sinter-resistant catalysts.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Carlson, E. (2019). Controlled Nanoparticle Deposition Reveals High-Temperature Stability and Density-Independent Sintering of Pd/SiO2 Emissions Control Catalysts. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/jg843jr5151. https://doi.org/10.25740/jg843jr5151.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
